  "Number": 1,
  "Question": "When the polynomial \\(x^3-2\\) is divided by the polynomial \\(x^2-2,\\) the remainder is",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(2\\)",
    "B": "\\(-2\\)",
    "C": "\\(-2x-2\\)",
    "D": "\\(2x+2\\)",
    "E": "\\(2x-2\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "E",
  "Solution": "Multiplying the divisor by \\(x\\) produces the leading term \\(x^3\\), so subtracting this multiple reveals the remainder:\n\\[x^3-2-x(x^2-2)=x^3-2-x^3+2x=2x-2.\\]\nThus\n\\[x^3-2=x(x^2-2)+(2x-2).\\]\nThe remaining polynomial has degree one, which is less than the divisor's degree two, so division is complete. The remainder is \\(2x-2\\), choice E.",
